    "text": "# **2.2 Stochastic reach-avoid problem** \n\nWe are interested in the terminal time problem [1]. As in [1], we seek open-loop control laws, to assure tractability (at the cost of conservativeness [4,10,11]). We define the _terminal time probability_ , _rx_<sup>_U_</sup> 0<sup>(</sup><sup>_S, T_),</sup> for a given initial state _x_ 0 _∈X_ and an open-loop control _U ∈U_<sup>_N_</sup> , as the probability that the state trajectory remains inside the safety set _S ⊆X_ and reaches the target set _T ⊆X_ at time _N_ , \n\n\n\nwith _R_ = _S_<sup>_N−_1</sup> _× T_ . The stochastic reach-avoid problem is formulated as: \n\n**Problem 1.** _Open-loop terminal time problem:_ \n\n\n\n_Problem 1 is equivalent to (see [1, Sec. 4]),_ \n\n\n\n_where z_ = 1 _T_ ( _xN_ )<sup>�</sup><sup>_N_</sup> _t_ =1<sup>_−_11</sup><sup>_S_(</sup><sup>_xt_) = 1</sup><sup>_R_(</sup><sup>_X_)</sup><sup>_is a Bernoulli random variable with a discrete probability measure_</sup> P<sup>_x_</sup> _z_<sup>0</sup><sup>_,U_</sup> _induced from_ P<sup>_x_</sup> _X_<sup>0</sup><sup>_,U_</sup> _._ \n\n**Remark 1.** _In Problem 1, p_<sup>_∗_</sup> ( _x_ 0) _is trivially zero when x_ 0 _̸ ∈S, irrespective of the choice of the controller._ \n\nIn [4,13], a mixed-integer linear program (MILP) was formulated as an approximation of Problem 1 when the safe and the target sets are _polytopic_ . Note that restriction of the safe and the target sets to polytopes is not severe since convex and compact sets admit tight polytopic underapproximations [19, Ex. 2.25]. We will denote the safe set _S_ , the target set _T_ , and the reach-avoid constraint set _R_ as \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwith _lS, lT ∈_ N _, L_ = ( _N −_ 1) _lS_ + _lT , fS ∈_ R<sup>_lS×nx_</sup> , _fT ∈_ R<sup>_lT ×nx_</sup> , and _F ∈_ R<sup>_L×nx_</sup> is constructed using _fS_ and _fT_ . Using the “big-M” approach [4,13,18], and a sampling-based empirical mean for _rx_<sup>_U_</sup> 0<sup>(</sup><sup>_S, T_)that</sup> replaces _W_<sup>_N_</sup> by a f